[FINAL-TEXT] All evidence is in. I have a complete picture of the fault.

**Summary of findings:**
- `router7.eth3` — assigned `10.0.0.1/24`, mapped to collision domain `link_wan_10_0_0` — is **DOWN** (no `UP/LOWER_UP` flags, `state DOWN`)
- FRR reports: *"eth3 is down / OSPF not enabled on this interface"*
- `router10.eth2` — the other end of the same segment, `10.0.0.2/24` — is **UP** but has zero OSPF neighbors toward router7
- Router7's OSPF area 0 shows **3 active interfaces / 3 full neighbors** (router1, router16, router8) — router10 is entirely absent
- Router7's RIB confirms `10.0.0.0/24` is now reached via the **alternate path** `eth5 → 172.25.1.2` at OSPF cost **[110/40]** instead of a directly connected [110/10]

